%T A397880 583570409348151704301241060407825784886854952578338514033828753668045366559490920902300523838376168661327976313850883828523840203589726368121250415134379
%N A397880 Numerator of c(n), the expected value of an entry at depth n in the Gilbreath (iterated absolute difference) array generated by independent standard exponential random variables.
%C A397880 The array is defined by a(0,j) = a_j and a(i+1,j) = |a(i,j) - a(i,j+1)|, where the a_j are independent standard exponential random variables; by stationarity E a(i,j) = c(i) depends only on the depth i.
%C A397880 Introduced by Chase, Hunter, and Tao, who computed c(0)..c(3) and proved Sum_{i=0..n} c(i) >= log(n+e), so c(n) cannot decay exponentially. Whether c(n) -> 0, or is even bounded, is open.
%C A397880 The sequence is not monotone: c(2) < c(3), and the exact values continue c(3) > c(4) < c(5) > c(6), tracking the binary digit sum A000120(n).
%C A397880 Empirically c(n) ~ C*lambda^A000120(n)/n, with lambda approximately 1.14-1.20 at accessible depths; see the Ross link.
%C A397880 c(4)-c(6) were computed in exact rational arithmetic; code and certificates are in the Ross GitHub link.
%H A397880 Z. Chase, Z. Hunter, and T. Tao, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08712">Gilbreath's conjecture: a Cramér random model and a deterministic analysis</a>, arXiv:2607.08712 [math.CO], 2026.
%H A397880 M. M. Ross, <a href="https://zenodo.org/records/21326025">Empirical structure of the Gilbreath decay constants</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A397880 M. M. Ross, <a href="https://github.com/michaelmross/Gilbreath">Gilbreath: code, data, and exact certificates</a>.
%H A397880 Terence Tao, <a href="https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2026/07/11/gilbreaths-conjecture-a-cramer-random-model-and-a-deterministic-analysis/">Gilbreath's conjecture: a Cramér random model and a deterministic analysis</a>, "What's New", Jul 11 2026 (comments section).
%H A397880 Terence Tao, <a href="https://teorth.github.io/tao-web/apps/gilbreath-cn.html">Gilbreath decay constants</a>.
%F A397880 c(n) = (n+1)*E[b(n,1)], where (b_1,...,b_{n+1}) is uniform on the standard simplex and b(n,1) is the apex of its absolute-difference array [Chase-Hunter-Tao].
%e A397880 c(0) = 1, c(1) = 1, c(2) = 7/9, c(3) = 227/288, c(4) = 778959731701/1447295850000 = 0.538217346302...
